const questions=[{q:'A business has next-year NOPAT of 100, a 10% cost of capital and a 10% marginal ROIC. What happens to value when stable growth rises from 2% to 4%, holding next-year NOPAT fixed?',options:['Value rises because earnings grow faster.','Value remains 1,000.','Value falls because reinvestment rises.'],answer:1,why:'At ROIC = WACC, growth is value neutral with forward NOPAT held fixed. V = NOPAT × (1 − g/ROIC) / (WACC − g) = NOPAT/WACC. Higher growth consumes exactly the value of additional future earnings.'},{q:'Revenue increases by 200 and incremental sales-to-capital is 2. After-tax operating income is 120. What is FCFF?',options:['120','100','20'],answer:2,why:'Reinvestment = 200/2 = 100. FCFF = 120 − 100 = 20. Revenue growth must be funded, even when the reported operating margin looks attractive.'},{q:'You are valuing a bank. Is subtracting deposits from an industrial FCFF enterprise value a reliable default?',options:['Yes, deposits are debt.','No. Funding is operational; use an equity and regulatory-capital model.','Yes, as long as WACC is low.'],answer:1,why:'Debt-like funding is part of a bank’s operations. Required capital, credit losses and distributable equity cash flows need explicit modeling. The industrial enterprise-to-equity bridge is generally unsuitable.'},{q:'A fiscal year ends January 31. Its annual report is filed March 15. Can those numbers be used for a February 1 historical valuation?',options:['Yes, because the fiscal period is complete.','No, unless they were already publicly disclosed in another verified source.','Yes, if the final numbers were accurate.'],answer:1,why:'Availability, not fiscal period end, determines the information set. A later restatement also cannot silently replace an original filing vintage.'},{q:'Consolidated FCFF includes 100% of a subsidiary. The parent owns 80%. What belongs in the equity bridge?',options:['Add the 20% noncontrolling interest.','Subtract the value of the 20% noncontrolling claim 